#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This guide demonstrates a minimal working example of the `qiskit-addon-obp` "
"package. We use operator backpropagation (OBP) to reduce the depth of a "
"quantum circuit by absorbing trailing gates into the observable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"An expectation value $\\langle O \\rangle = \\langle \\psi | U^\\dagger O U "
"| \\psi \\rangle$ is unchanged if we peel a trailing block of gates off the "
"circuit $U$ and conjugate the observable by it classically. OBP applies this"
" repeatedly, evaluating part of the circuit classically so that only a "
"shallower circuit runs on hardware. The price is that each absorbed gate can"
" expand the observable into more Pauli terms, so the depth saved must be "
"weighed against the growth of the observable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To see examples of how to build realistic workflows with this tool and "
"execute on quantum hardware, check out the tutorials on the IBM Quantum "
"Platform ([OBP Tutorial](/tutorials/operator-back-propagation)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Prepare inputs for OBP {#prepare-inputs-for-obp}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"OBP takes as input a list of circuit slices and an observable. It "
"backpropagates slices one at a time from the end of the circuit into the "
"observable, reducing circuit depth at the cost of additional Pauli terms in "
"the observable. Here we generate a time-evolution circuit for a 10-qubit "
"Heisenberg model and slice it by gate type.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Below we draw the original circuit, then the same circuit recombined with "
"barriers marking the slice boundaries — each slice is a unit that can be "
"absorbed into the observable in a single backpropagation step.\n"
msgstr ""

